  What it does (following crtm_aaod_fm_intro.md):
  - CRTM_AOD, CRTM_AOD_TL, CRTM_AOD_AD and CRTM_AOD_K take an optional FINE_MODE
    argument, default .FALSE., which can be combined with AAOD. With it on,
    each aerosol type's concentration is multiplied by its fmass1, linearly
    interpolated in RH:
    - dust, sea salt and nitrate bin by bin;
    - sulfate, OC1/OC2 and BC1/BC2 as species.
  - The TL, AD and K-matrix include the derivative with respect to RH.
  - fmass1 is stored in AerosolCoeff. A netCDF coefficient file can supply it as
    a variable fmass1(n_Types, n_RH); the shape is checked. Otherwise, and
    always for binary files, the defaults are used.

  The default values:
  - They live in a new module,
    src/Coefficients/AerosolCoeff/AerosolCoeff_fmass1_Default.f90. It has one
    table of 36 RH values per CRTM type, each labelled with the GMAO file and
    entry it comes from, and is generated directly from the six .nc4 files.
  - Types are matched by name.
  - Values within 1e-12 of 1 are stored as exactly 1.
  - Both CRTM sulfate types use the single optics_SU entry; that's the one
    choice your instructions don't settle.

  Tests:
  - Unit tests Unit_AerosolScatter_FineMode_{TL,AD,K}: each checks AOD and AAOD
    on a profile with all 19 types. One layer is at RH 0.935, so every
    RH-dependent factor is exercised. The TL test also checks all 19 types
    against values I read by hand from the GMAO files at RH 0.80 and 0.99.
  - Regression tests test_FineMode_AOD and test_FineMode_AAOD, forward and
    K-matrix, for six sensors:
    - Profile 1: Dust 1, Sea salt 2, Sulfate 1.
    - Profile 2: Nitrate 1, Organic carbon 2, Dust 2.

    RH goes up to 0.98.

